      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the sensation wouldn't be as distinct as simply moving air and feeling your throat shake, I'll try to explain it technically. For the unvoiced /s/, the vocal cords don't vibrate, so you mostly just feel the air passing over them. However, when producing the voiced /z/, there's additional tension and vibration in the vocal cords, which you might feel as a subtle vibration or even a change in pressure in the throat if examined closely. It's important to note that physical sensations can be subjective and not all learners might be able to feel this clearly due to the small size and precise control of the vocal cords.",
