    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so the \"noise\" part is really important. i feel like the /s/ sounds almost like a hiss or a sharp air sound, while the /z/ is more of a continuous, low hum. do linguists use any specific terms to describe the quality of that \"noise\" or the way those sounds feel? like is there a way to categorize the \"texture\" of the sound?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Linguists indeed have terms to categorize the quality of fricatives. One such term is \"sonority hierarchy,\" which refers to a scale of openness of the vocal tract, frequency, and amplification features. Fricatives like /s/ and /z/ have high sonority—the /s/ is higher in the hierarchy due to its higher frequency and relatively noisier nature, often referred to as \"hissing.\" Conversely, the /z/ is lower in sonority due to the involvement of the vocal cords providing a buzz and a continuous voice quality. Descriptors like \"nasal\" can be used, as /s/ is more like a \"thin, sharp hiss,\" whereas /z/ is described as a \"fuller, buzzing lower noise\" or \"voiced hiss,\" to better capture their distinctive qualities.",
      "source": "target",
